Description: CVE-2026-82560 - Handle pathological margins in Pod::Text
 Pod::Text and its subclasses (Pod::Text::Color, Pod::Text::Overstrike and
 Pod::Text::Termcap, all shipped in the vendored cpan/podlators) computed the
 available wrapping width as opt_width minus the current margin.  When a POD
 document requested an =over indentation that, added to the baseline text
 indentation, equalled or exceeded the configured output width, the width
 became zero or negative.  The wrapping regular expression could then match
 zero characters successfully, causing the wrap loop to spin forever and hang
 the formatter on attacker-supplied POD input (denial of service).
 .
 This backports the upstream fix: when the effective width is not positive,
 the formatter reports a "Margin is wider than the output width" warning via
 the standard POD error mechanism and forces the margin to zero so the text is
 still emitted.  The guard is added to all four wrap() implementations.  A new
 regression test (t/text/wrap-margin.t) exercises every formatter with a
 pathological margin and asserts that formatting terminates and reports the
 error.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/rra/podlators/commit/70510174f69eb54aa6d617bde4e1402cd9b7c61f
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/rra/podlators/commit/092070954c04cffeb6ffd57a78d796eed2f66e9c
Author: Russ Allbery <rra@cpan.org>
Last-Update: 2026-09-22
---
--- a/cpan/podlators/lib/Pod/Text.pm
+++ b/cpan/podlators/lib/Pod/Text.pm
@@ -238,6 +238,17 @@ sub wrap {
     my $output = '';
     my $spaces = ' ' x $$self{MARGIN};
     my $width = $$self{opt_width} - $$self{MARGIN};
+
+    # Pathological margins make wrapping impossible.  In this case, complain
+    # to the user and reset the margin to 0.
+    if ($width <= 0) {
+        my $error = 'Margin is wider than the output width';
+        $self->whine($self->line_count(), $error);
+        $spaces = q{};
+        $width = $$self{opt_width};
+    }
+
+    # Perform the wrapping.
     while (length > $width) {
         if (s/^([^\n]{0,$width})[ \t\n]+// || s/^([^\n]{$width})//) {
             $output .= $spaces . $1 . "\n";
@@ -948,6 +959,13 @@ and the input file it was given could not be opened.
